EXPERIMENT 13: GREATEST COMMON DIVISOR (GCD) USING JUNIT ASSERTIONS: GREATEST COMMON DIVISOR (GCD) USING JUNIT ASSERTIONS

## 1. AIM

To write a Java program to calculate the Greatest Common Divisor (GCD) of two numbers and validate the output using JUnit assertions.

## 2. ALGORITHM

1. Start the program.
2. Read two integer numbers.
3. Check whether both numbers are valid.
4. Find the remainder by dividing the first number by the second number.
5. Repeat the process until the remainder becomes zero.
6. The last non-zero divisor is the GCD.
7. Use JUnit assertions to validate the calculated result.
8. Display the output and stop the program.

## 3. CODE

### File 1: GCD.java

```java
public class GCD {

    public static int findGCD(int a, int b) {

        a = Math.abs(a);
        b = Math.abs(b);

        while (b != 0) {
            int temp = b;
            b = a % b;
            a = temp;
        }

        return a;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int a = 24;
        int b = 36;

        System.out.println("GCD of " + a + " and " + b
                + " is: " + findGCD(a, b));
    }
}
```

### File 2: GCDTest.java

```java
import org.junit.jupiter.api.Test;
import static org.junit.jupiter.api.Assertions.*;

public class GCDTest {

    @Test
    public void testGCD() {
        assertEquals(12, GCD.findGCD(24, 36));
    }

    @Test
    public void testEqualNumbers() {
        assertEquals(10, GCD.findGCD(10, 10));
    }

    @Test
    public void testOneNumberIsZero() {
        assertEquals(15, GCD.findGCD(15, 0));
    }

    @Test
    public void testNegativeNumbers() {
        assertEquals(6, GCD.findGCD(-18, 24));
    }
}
```

